 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** Renders 800x800 images at 167.68 FPS on an NVIDIA V100 GPU, which is over 3000 times faster than conventional NeRFs (approx. 30 seconds per image) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory:** Memory usage is reduced by using a sparse octree structure and pruning empty voxels. The exact memory footprint depends on the scene complexity and SH degree, but it is significantly lower than dense grid-based methods like Instant NGP for high-resolution scenes [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering capability; preserves view-dependent effects (specularities) via SH; compatible with web technologies for browser-based interactive rendering [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires an initial training phase (NeRF-SH) which is slower than training a standard NeRF; quality depends on the SH degree and pruning threshold; not suitable for dynamic scenes without retraining [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When real-time or interactive rendering of static scenes with complex view-dependent effects is required.
-*   **When not to use:** For dynamic scenes, or when training time is a critical constraint and offline rendering is acceptable.
+- **Time Complexity:** Rendering is significantly faster than standard NeRF. Inference times are approximately 3000 times faster than the original NeRF implementation [S1].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Uses a sparse octree to reduce memory requirements compared to dense grids [S2, unofficial].
+- **Measured Performance:** Renders 800x800 images at 167.68 FPS on an NVIDIA V100 GPU [S2, unofficial]. This is over 3000 times faster than conventional NeRFs (approx. 30 seconds per image) [S2, unofficial].
+- **Scaling:** Not given in the sources.
+- **Strengths:** Real-time performance without sacrificing quality; preserves ability to perform free-viewpoint rendering of scenes with arbitrary geometry and view-dependent effects; amenable to modern web technologies [S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Requires an initial training phase (NeRF-SH or Monte Carlo estimation) and fine-tuning; quality depends on the number of SH bands used [S2, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** When real-time rendering of view-dependent NeRFs is required [S2, unofficial].
+- **When not:** When interactive training is preferred over pre-tabulated rendering; when extreme view-dependent effects require very high SH bands (memory intensive) [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-PlenOctrees were introduced by Alex Yu et al. from UC Berkeley in March 2021 [S2, unofficial]. The method is named after plenoptic functions, referencing the work of Adelson and Bergen [S2, unofficial]. It was developed concurrently with other real-time NeRF acceleration methods such as FastNeRF and KiloNeRF [S1]. The approach has influenced subsequent work, including the adaptation of spherical harmonics in Gaussian Splatting [S1].
+PlenOctrees were introduced by Alex Yu et al. in March 2021 [S1]. The method is named after plenoptic functions [S2, unofficial]. It was developed to overcome the slowness of naive NeRF rendering, which requires dense sampling and neural network inference for every sample dependent on viewing direction [S2, unofficial]. The approach was concurrent with other acceleration methods like FastNeRF and KiloNeRF [S1][S3, unofficial].
 
